warchest, or continued Turek momentum fueled by outside spending, would break the current dead heat.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Joni Ernst (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Joni Ernst's retirement leaves an open Senate seat in a state that has drifted Republican, and the contest between Ashley Hinson and Josh Turek sits at the center of the midterm map. Averaged recent polling puts Turek narrowly ahead, though Republican-affiliated pollsters show Hinson leading, and while nine forecasters call the race a Tossup, one rates it Tilt Republican. Prediction markets give Hinson a slight edge.\n\nHinson holds a clear financial advantage, with more than $6.7 million on hand to Turek's $2.4 million, but outside groups have poured roughly $9.8 million behind the Democrat. The national environment favors Democrats this cycle, while Iowa's partisan lean works against Turek, leaving the two forces roughly balanced. The race stands as a genuine tossup with a faint Democratic tilt, and either candidate could prevail.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6012, "margin": 1.25}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5867, "margin": 0.8}]}